ons (muscle weakness, neuropathies)", "Systemic inflammation markers (elevated BP 177/108 mmHg and low-grade fever 37.5°C)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Brachial Plexopathy", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.2854648158377732, "posterior": 0.30027553372945215, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The patient's symptoms (dull aching/burning pain) are more typical of brachial plexopathy (B1) than steroid-induced neuropathy (B5), which often presents with lancinating or paresthesic pain patterns."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of trauma/radiation history weakens brachial plexopathy (B1) since traumatic causes are common in this group, but doesn't fully exclude it (weak effect)."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Are there signs of radiation or trauma history?", "Does physical exam suggest a localized vs. diffuse process?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Electromyography (EMG) of upper limb", "Imaging (CT/MRI) of brachial plexus and upper thoracic spine"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Thoracic Neoplasm with Neurological Compromise", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.14433614283932356, "posterior": 0.15182470806545334, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Unintentional weight loss (17lbs in 1mo) and smoking history (40+ pack-years) are significant alarm features for thoracic neoplasm (B2), moderately increasing its likelihood."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of chronic cough, hemoptysis, or chest pain?", "Are there paraneoplastic symptoms unrelated to limb deficits?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Chest CT scan", "Tumor markers (e.g., Ca 125, CEA)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Vascular Compression of Upper Limb", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.04558490000187437, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Inflammatory / Autoimmune Disease", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.019943393750820042, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of trauma/radiation history weakens brachial plexopathy (B1) since traumatic causes are common in this group, but doesn't fully exclude it (weak effect)."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Metabolic / Steroid-Induced Neuropathy", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.00890330078161609, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The patient's symptoms (dull aching/burning pain) are more typical of brachial plexopathy (B1) than steroid-induced neuropathy (B5), which often presents with lancinating or paresthesic pain patterns."]}, "B6": {"id": "B6", "label": "Neurodegenerative / Systemic Neurological Process", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.021367921875878614, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.16037349204369283, "posterior": 0.1686941200727259, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Weight loss and systemic inflammation weakly support paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy (B1.1), but the absence of specific paraneoplastic symptoms like Lambert-Eaton prevents strong evidence."], "evidence_against": ["No paraneoplastic symptoms in the vignette weakens small cell cancer's role as the underlying neoplasm for B1.1."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of chronic cough, hemoptysis, or apical mass symptoms?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Chest CT with contrast to assess for apical/peripheral tumors", "Tumor markers (e.g., CEA, Ca125)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "etiology", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Traumatic/Repetitive Injury Brachial Plexopathy", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.07697927618097257, "posterior": 0.08097317763490845, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Bodybuilding suggests repetitive injury possibility supporting B1.2, but lack of trauma documentation weakens this link."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of documented trauma scenarios (falls/weightlifting activities) reduces the probability of traumatic plexopathy (B1.2) by eliminating primary traumatic mechanisms."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the patient report a specific injury (e.g., fall, lifting incident)?", "History of recent heavy weight-training activities?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["MRI brachial plexus for focal structural lesions", "Neuron-specific enolase testing if metabolic injury is suspected"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.1, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "mechanism", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Radiation-Induced Brachial Plexopathy", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04811204761310785, "posterior": 0.05060823602181777, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of prior cervical/thoracic radiation therapy?", "Is there a temporal link to radiation exposure in the patient's timeline?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Review of prior radiation treatments if available", "PET-CT for metabolic tumor activity assessment"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 0.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.05, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "mechanism", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.038489638090486285, "posterior": 0.040486588817454226, "danger": 0.95, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["No paraneoplastic symptoms in the vignette weakens small cell cancer's role as the underlying neoplasm for B1.1.", "Missing classical paraneoplastic features (back pain/visual changes) directly lowers credibility of small cell lung cancer in this presentation."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of paraneoplastic symptoms (e.g., Lambert-Eaton, opsoclonus)?", "Are there systemic features of SCLC like weight loss or lymphadenopathy?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["ProGRP (small cell lung cancer tumour marker)", "Chest CT with emphasis on central hilar/apical features"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 1.0, "level_role": "etiology", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Non-Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Brachial Plexus Invasion", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.06735686665835099, "posterior": 0.07085153043054487, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Smoking duration/weight loss patterns suggest indolent vs rapid disease progression?", "Is there a palpable mediastinal mass or pleural effusion on exam?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CEA tumour marker", "Chest MRI for evaluating brachial plexus invasion"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.4, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "etiology", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Metastatic Thoracic Tumour to Brachial Plexus (e.g., Melanoma, Breast Cancer)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.038489638090486285, "posterior": 0.040486588817454226, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Any known primary cancer history?", "Systemic symptoms of other primaries (e.g., breast tenderness, melanoma skin lesions)?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["PET-CT for primary cancer identification", "Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), cancer antigen 153"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.3, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "phase_crossing", "classification_axis": "etiology"}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's combination of unintentional weight loss (17lbs), subacute neurological symptoms, and chronic systemic inflammation suggest paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy (B1.1) as the underlying mechanism rather than direct radiation injury or idiopathic causes?", "result_summary": "Weight loss and systemic inflammation weakly support paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy (B1.1), but the absence of specific paraneoplastic symptoms like Lambert-Eaton prevents strong evidence.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "moderate_for", "B2.1": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's bodybuilding lifestyle and heavy weightlifting history provide sufficient evidence to support traumatic/repetitive injury brachial plexopathy (B1.2) as the mechanism?", "result_summary": "Bodybuilding suggests repetitive injury possibility supporting B1.2, but lack of trauma documentation weakens this link.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.2": "moderate_for"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of paraneoplastic symptoms like Lambert-Eaton syndrome, opsoclonus, or lymphadenopathy diminish the likelihood of small cell lung carcinoma as the neoplastic basis for paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy (B1.1)?", "result_summary": "No paraneoplastic symptoms in the vignette weakens small cell cancer's role as the underlying neoplasm for B1.1.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "moderate_against", "B2.1": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of documented acute trauma incidents or specific heavy weightlifting activities in the vignette inconsistent with a traumatic brachial plexopathy (B1.2)?", "result_summary": "Lack of documented trauma scenarios (falls/weightlifting activities) reduces the probability of traumatic plexopathy (B1.2) by eliminating primary traumatic mechanisms.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.2": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of specific paraneoplastic features (back pain, blurry vision, skin discoloration) from the vignette make small cell lung carcinoma with paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy (B2.1) less likely than other thoracic neoplasms?", "result_summary": "Missing classical paraneoplastic features (back pain/visual changes) directly lowers credibility of small cell lung cancer in this presentation.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.1": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute/progressive unilateral deficit with constitutional features (weight loss, systemic inflammation) argue for brachial plexopathy (B1) over vascular/inflammatory etiologies?", "result_summary": "The subacute progressive right arm weakness with constitutional features (weight loss, systemic inflammation) and sensory deficits argues in favor of brachial plexopathy (B1) due to its characteristic presentation pattern, while reducing likelihood in the broader group of vascular/inflammatory causes (B3 and family)."}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.2698832527502149, "B2": 0.1728465776040702, "B3": 0.05121380077157635, "B4": 0.017924830270051727, "B5": 0.008002156370558806, "B6": 0.019205175289341132, "B1.1": 0.15161980491585106, "B1.2": 0.07277750635960852, "B1.3": 0.04548594147475531, "B2.1": 0.07277750635960852, "B2.2": 0.06368031806465743, "B2.3": 0.03638875317980426}, {"B1": 0.2854648158377732, "B2": 0.14433614283932356, "B3": 0.054170601756980685, "B4": 0.018959710614943243, "B5": 0.008464156524528234, "B6": 0.020313975658867757, "B1.1": 0.16037349204369283, "B1.2": 0.07697927618097257, "B1.3": 0.04811204761310785, "B2.1": 0.038489638090486285, "B2.2": 0.06735686665835099, "B2.3": 0.038489638090486285}, {"B1": 0.30027553372945215, "B2": 0.15182470806545334, "B3": 0.04558490000187437, "B4": 0.019943393750820042, "B5": 0.00890330078161609, "B6": 0.021367921875878614, "B1.1": 0.1686941200727259, "B1.2": 0.08097317763490845, "B1.3": 0.05060823602181777, "B2.1": 0.040486588817454226, "B2.2": 0.07085153043054487, "B2.3": 0.040486588817454226}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers or time-critical conditions present.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "No branch has reached posterior probability >= 0.75. Dangerous alternatives (B1.1: paraneoplastic brachial plexopathy, danger 0.9; B2.1: SCLC, danger 0.95) remain unresolved with posterior probabilities >= 0.10. Key discriminators like chest CT for B2.2/B2.3 and EMG for B1/traumatic etiology have not been ordered."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.55, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.1686941200727259, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm. The patient states he has experienced these symptoms for a few weeks; however, today his hand felt so weak he dropped his cup of tea. The patient has a medical history of diabetes. He drinks 2-7 alcoholic drinks per day and has smoked 2 packs of cigarettes per day since he was 25. The patient uses anabolic steroids. He has lost 17 pounds since he last came to the emergency department 1 month ago. His temperature is 99.5°F (37.5°C), blood pressure is 177/108 mmHg, pulse is 90/min, respirations are 17/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam reveals decreased sensation in the right arm and 2/5 strength in the right arm and 5/5 strength in the left arm. The patient states that he is experiencing a dull aching and burning pain in his right arm during the exam. Symptoms are not changed with changes in head and neck position.", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Apical lung tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Brachial plexopathy"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Cerebral infarction"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Scalenus anticus syndrome"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Subclavian steal syndrome"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: weakness of right arm worsening to dropping objects", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Symptom duration: 2-4 weeks", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Medical history: diabetes", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Alcohol use: 2-7 drinks/day", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Tobacco use: 2 pack/day since age 25", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Anabolic steroid use", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "direct", "content": "Weight loss: 17 pounds since 1 month ago", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E8", "kind": "direct", "content": "Temperature: 99.5°F (37.5°C)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E9", "kind": "direct", "content": "Blood pressure: 177/108 mmHg", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E10", "kind": "direct", "content": "Pulse: 90/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E11", "kind": "direct", "content": "Respirations: 17/min", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E12", "kind": "direct", "content": "Oxygen saturation: 98% on room air", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E13", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical exam: decreased sensation in right arm", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E14", "kind": "direct", "content": "Muscle strength: right arm 2/5, left arm 5/5", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E15", "kind": "direct", "content": "Pain description: dull aching and burning in right arm", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E16", "kind": "direct", "content": "Neurological findings: symptoms unchanged with head/neck position", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'fits', 'Abnormality of the brachial nerve plexus', 'Squamous cell lung carcinoma', 'brachial plexopathy', 'Brachial plexus neuropathy', 'Small cell lung carcinoma'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=67%, source=both]\n\nParaneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy vs Radiation-Induced Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nParaneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy vs Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, back pain, blurry vision, bone pain, changes in skin color\n\nParaneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy vs Non-Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Brachial Plexus Invasion:\n  Favours first:  burning sensation, deformity of the arm or hand, difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, hand, or fingers, diminished arm reflexes, electric shock-like sensation\n  Favours second: bone pain, chest pain, chronic cough, coughing up blood, dizziness\n\nRadiation-Induced Brachial Plexopathy vs Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant inheritance, back pain, blurry vision, bone pain, changes in skin color\n\nRadiation-Induced Brachial Plexopathy vs Non-Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Brachial Plexus Invasion:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n  Favours second: bone pain, chest pain, chronic cough, coughing up blood, dizziness\n\nSmall Cell Lung Carcinoma with Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy vs Non-Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Brachial Plexus Invasion:\n  Favours first:  autosomal dominant inheritance, back pain, blurry vision, changes in skin color, chest pain or discomfort\n  Favours second: chest pain, dizziness, large cell lung carcinoma, lung adenocarcinoma, numbness or weakness in an arm or leg\n\nRelated to Radiation-Induced Brachial Plexopathy: acquired peripheral neuropathy, nerve plexus disease, radiation-induced disorder\n\nRelated to Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Paraneoplastic Brachial Plexopathy: bronchopulmonary tumor, combined small cell lung carcinoma, lung carcinoma\n\nRelated to Non-Small Cell Lung Carcinoma with Brachial Plexus Invasion: lung adenocarcinoma, lung carcinoma, lung large cell carcinoma\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 55-year-old male bodybuilder presents to the emergency department with weakness of his right arm → hyporeflexia of upper limbs → Radiation-Induced Brachial Plexopathy"}
