 infection in lab results", "Neoplastic Colonic Lesion (chronic presentation more typical for cancer): Age 43, no alarm signs (weight loss/obstruction/constitutional fatigue), and subacute onset suggest lower priority for malignancy"], "alarm_features": []}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Colorectal Traumatic Injury", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.11902624220814201, "posterior": 0.1303151154299837, "danger": 0.3, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["The gradual increase in rectal bleeding over multiple days aligns more with vascular malformation pathophysiology, where intermittent bleeding from an abnormal vessel can accumulate over time without acute trauma presentation.", "The normal rectal exam (no trauma/straining) contradicts traumatic injury but doesn't definitively prove vascular origin. This pattern is more consistent with non-mechanical bleed sources like vascular malformations."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of physical injury to the perineum or anal region?", "Recent episodes of forceful straining or vomiting? "], "requestable_discriminators": ["Digital rectal exam for signs of trauma", "Colonoscopy to visualize rectal/colonic structures"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Colorectal Vascular Malformation", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.25210989405638357, "posterior": 0.27602089535471547, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The gradual increase in rectal bleeding over multiple days aligns more with vascular malformation pathophysiology, where intermittent bleeding from an abnormal vessel can accumulate over time without acute trauma presentation.", "The normal rectal exam (no trauma/straining) contradicts traumatic injury but doesn't definitively prove vascular origin. This pattern is more consistent with non-mechanical bleed sources like vascular malformations."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia history slightly reduces the probability of vascular malformation, but sporadic cases can still present without family history. The overall vascular likelihood remains elevated relative to trauma."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of recurrent painless rectal bleeding in the past?", "Previous diagnosis of hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Osler-Weber-Rendu syndrome)?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Colonoscopy", "Angiography to detect vascular lesions"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.04886816828624387, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of traditional IBD alarm features suggests against this differential, though atypical presentations remain possible. This weakly favors vascular/injury differentials over inflammatory pathology."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Colonic Diverticular Disease", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.0814469471437398, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Colorectal Neoplasm with Subacute Hemorrhage", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.0407234735718699, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B6": {"id": "B6", "label": "Acute Upper Gastrointestinal Bleed with Lower Rectal Presentation", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.016289389428747962, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Sporadic Colorectal Arteriovenous Malformation", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.1455934393079818, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Alcohol-induced vomiting with elevated intra-abdominal pressure is less likely to directly cause rectal vessel trauma compared to external physical injury mechanisms. This slightly reduces the likelihood of B2.1 (sporadic AVM) as pressure changes without prior vascular anomalies would be unlikely."]}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Heredity-Associated Vascular Lesion (HHT-1 / HHT-2 subtypes)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.054597539740493174, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Alcohol-induced vomiting does not directly contradict hereditary HHT but is not a typical clinical feature, leading to a weak reduction in HHT subtype probability."]}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Colorectal Angiodysplasia without Hereditary Syndrome", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.07582991630624052, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Colorectal Traumatic Injury from External Physical Trauma", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.070169677539222, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of documented external injury or straining eliminates a major mechanism for traumatic injury. This supports closure of B1.1 (external trauma) and maintains skepticism about traumatic explanations."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Traumatic Injury from Forceful Vomiting/Middle Esophageal Strain", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0601454378907617, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["While forceful vomiting can cause Mallory-Weiss tears in the upper GI tract, the rectal bleeding location contradicts esophageal injury as the primary source. This weakens B1.2 (traumatic vomiting) as the explanatory model."]}}, "frontier": [], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 2, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of recurrent, stress-related vomiting (alcohol-induced) argue against hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia subtypes as the underlying cause?", "result_summary": "Alcohol-induced vomiting does not directly contradict hereditary HHT but is not a typical clinical feature, leading to a weak reduction in HHT subtype probability.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.2": "moderate_against", "B2.1": "neutral", "B1": "neutral", "B2": "moderate_against", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the patient’s history of alcohol-induced vomiting have indirectly caused trauma to rectal vessels through prolonged intra-abdominal pressure elevation?", "result_summary": "Alcohol-induced vomiting with elevated intra-abdominal pressure is less likely to directly cause rectal vessel trauma compared to external physical injury mechanisms. This slightly reduces the likelihood of B2.1 (sporadic AVM) as pressure changes without prior vascular anomalies would be unlikely.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.1": "weak_against", "B1.2": "neutral", "B1": "neutral", "B2.2": "neutral", "B2.3": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would the temporal relationship between repeated vomiting and subsequent hematochezia support a traumatic injury mechanism via vascular wall strain?", "result_summary": "Recurrent vomiting with rectal bleeding over multiple days is more consistent with vascular malformation pathophysiology than direct trauma. Timing supports B2.3 (angiodysplasia) over traumatic causes (B1) and aligns better with intermittent bleeding patterns.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.2": "weak_against", "B1": "weak_against", "B2.3": "neutral", "B2.1": "neutral", "B2.2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of perineal anal trauma or documented straining/weightlifting in the prior 24 hrs argue against external physical trauma as the source of rectal bleeding?", "result_summary": "Lack of documented external injury or straining eliminates a major mechanism for traumatic injury. This supports closure of B1.1 (external trauma) and maintains skepticism about traumatic explanations.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.1": "weak_against", "B1": "weak_against", "B2": "neutral", "B2.1": "neutral", "B2.2": "neutral", "B2.3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 4, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the clinical consistency between esophageal straining from forceful vomiting and rectal blood loss argue against B1.2 as the primary mechanism?", "result_summary": "While forceful vomiting can cause Mallory-Weiss tears in the upper GI tract, the rectal bleeding location contradicts esophageal injury as the primary source. This weakens B1.2 (traumatic vomiting) as the explanatory model.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1.2": "weak_against", "B1": "weak_against", "B2.3": "neutral", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal leukocyte count and lack of systemic inflammation (no fever/leukocytosis) rule out active inflammatory bowel disease as a dangerous differential?", "result_summary": "The normal leukocyte count and absence of systemic inflammation (no fever/leukocytosis) do not definitively rule out inflammatory bowel disease (B3) as a differential diagnosis, but they reduce suspicion for an active inflammatory process as the primary cause of rectal bleeding. This evidence weakly supports exclusion of IBD while maintaining its status as a low-probability consideration."}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.14436838830118232, "B2": 0.288985687616677, "B3": 0.05413814561294335, "B4": 0.07218419415059114, "B5": 0.03609209707529557, "B6": 0.01443683883011823, "B2.1": 0.16129433727442438, "B2.2": 0.06048537647790914, "B2.3": 0.06720597386434349, "B1.1": 0.0777368244698674, "B1.2": 0.0666315638313149}, {"B1": 0.11902624220814201, "B2": 0.25210989405638357, "B3": 0.05579355103506655, "B4": 0.07439140138008875, "B5": 0.037195700690044374, "B6": 0.014878280276017751, "B2.1": 0.13298104301875177, "B2.2": 0.04986789113203192, "B2.3": 0.06926095990559988, "B1.1": 0.06409105349669186, "B1.2": 0.05493518871145015}, {"B1": 0.1303151154299837, "B2": 0.27602089535471547, "B3": 0.04886816828624387, "B4": 0.0814469471437398, "B5": 0.0407234735718699, "B6": 0.016289389428747962, "B2.1": 0.1455934393079818, "B2.2": 0.054597539740493174, "B2.3": 0.07582991630624052, "B1.1": 0.070169677539222, "B1.2": 0.0601454378907617}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers are present. Patient has stable vital signs (no hypotension, tachycardia with end-organ compromise, or respiratory compromise). Hemoglobin remains within normal limits. Differential focuses on subacute rectal bleeding with low probability of life-threatening conditions.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "